        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 716,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Marks the elements that are ordinary, finite numbers - not nan (\"not a number\") and not infinite - and counts them.\n\nInfinity comes from dividing by zero, from exp of something large, from log of 0;\nnan from 0 divided by 0 and other calculations with no answer. Either one wrecks what follows: a plot's range, a mean, a filter's memory. np.isfinite gives a mask of the values you can trust, ready for np.where (keep the good, replace the rest) or np.all (is this whole frame usable?).\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.isfinite\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a message can say nan and inf). Every input is tested and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue where the element is an ordinary number, the same shape as the input.\n\ncount:\nHow many elements are ordinary numbers. Sent before the mask.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0: nan, inf and -inf all fail, so the mask is true false true false false true and the count is 3. A metro re-sends the message five times a second; edit it and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isnan marks only the nans. np.nan_to_num replaces nan and infinity with numbers you choose. np.all of the mask says whether every value is good.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 700,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Marks the elements that are ordinary, finite numbers - not nan (\"not a number\") and not infinite - and counts them.\n\nInfinity comes from dividing by zero, from exp of something large, from log of 0;\nnan from 0 divided by 0 and other calculations with no answer. Either one wrecks what follows: a plot's range, a mean, a filter's memory. np.isfinite gives a mask of the values you can trust, ready for np.where (keep the good, replace the rest) or np.all (is this whole frame usable?).\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.isfinite\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a message can say nan and inf). Every input is tested and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue where the element is an ordinary number, the same shape as the input.\n\ncount:\nHow many elements are ordinary numbers. Sent before the mask.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 nan 3 inf -inf 0: nan, inf and -inf all fail, so the mask is true false true false false true and the count is 3. A metro re-sends the message five times a second; edit it and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isnan marks only the nans. np.nan_to_num replaces nan and infinity with numbers you choose. np.all of the mask says whether every value is good."
        },
